TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) — Space Mission Design","overview":"Design a preliminary space mission that benefits Australia and report its mission concept and space-segment design. Apply course knowledge to explain the spacecraft, launch, orbit, communications, operations and end-of-life plan, using mission-design software and, where useful, hand calculations."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale (primary mission, need, number of spacecraft and rationale, operator and customers)","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard, with detailed discussion and appropriate literature references."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements; descriptions may lack detail and/or complete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are unaddressed; descriptions or referencing are poor or lacking."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design (design method and evidence; launch site, vehicle and rationale; orbit and elements; maneuvers; ground control and communication frequency; mission duration and EOL)","weight":60,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Shows strong understanding of the design procedure and a design clearly aligned with the mission and course concepts. Addresses all major requirements comprehensively, accurately and persuasively, supported by quality evidence such as software screenshots/files, hand calculations and literature references."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Shows adequate understanding of the design procedure. The design may be inconsistent in purpose or clarity, or contain errors. Addresses major requirements but may lack detail or evidence; content may be incomplete or unpersuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Shows minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are unaddressed and/or unsupported; content is incomplete or unpersuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Reaches detailed, appropriate conclusions from the report evidence."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Reaches some conclusions, but they may be generic or lack detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Reaches no useful conclusions."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Uses a clear, logical engineering-report structure and is easy to read, well written, grammatically sound and appropriately styled. Formatting is consistent; the executive summary or abstract concisely summarizes the report; figures and tables are clear and appropriate; in-text citations and reference list match and are correctly cited."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Uses headings and subheadings but is not fully professional and may be difficult to read. Writing communicates the broad idea but has some spelling, grammar or style flaws. Figures and tables are labelled but formatting may be unclear or inconsistent, causing loss of context. In-text citations and reference list match and are mostly correct."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Poorly structured or incoherent, or shows misunderstanding of section purposes. Difficult to read due to poor writing, many spelling or grammar errors, or inappropriate style. Poor presentation impedes reading (for example, inconsistent formatting or unlabelled figures/tables); references are absent or inconsistently cited."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report says the near-polar orbit provides a constant supply of sunlight. A 600 km sun-synchronous orbit can have eclipse periods; sunlight availability depends on beta angle and season."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The table says the initial RAAN indicates the launch-facility location. RAAN specifies the orbital plane's orientation relative to the reference direction; it is not determined by the launch site's location alone."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant is identified as a radio observatory, but the report does not establish that it has suitable satellite TT&C or data-downlink equipment, frequencies, or a control capability."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access output contains a repeated access entry and an apparent date/time wrap or discontinuity. The report does not reconcile these features before using the listed periods to support its contact statistics."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"No sensor swath, ground-sample distance, pointing limits, or image-quality analysis is provided to show that a 600 km orbit and proposed payload can image the reef adequately."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The drag-sail proposal has no area, decay-time estimate, or demonstration that the spacecraft will meet an applicable post-mission disposal timeframe."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"The bibliography cites Wikipedia, which the task explicitly disallows, and several numbered citations appearing in the launch tables are not matched by bibliography entries."}],"overall":"The report presents a coherent preliminary single-satellite concept and makes useful use of STK, but several design claims lack supporting analysis. In particular, payload performance, ground-station communications capability and EOL decay are not demonstrated; orbit interpretation and referencing also need correction.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The reef-monitoring mission and its Australian benefit are explained, and the report justifies using one spacecraft. Likely users and funding sources are identified, but the operator is not clearly specified. The rationale is useful but does not establish detailed mission performance or cite all claims with strong sources.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The spacecraft is intended to image and send data to ground stations for collation and analysis."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report addresses launch, orbit, communications, mission duration and EOL, and includes STK output and visualisations. However, key design evidence is incomplete: imaging performance and reef coverage are not quantified, the proposed ground facility's spacecraft-communications capability is not established, and EOL disposal is not timed or analysed. Some orbit and access interpretations are incorrect.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."},{"where":"Image 4","quote":""}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the selected launch, orbit and ground station and connects the design to the mission purpose. It is mostly a summary rather than a detailed evaluation of evidence, limitations or unresolved risks.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a logical structure, an executive summary, labelled sections and useful figures. Writing and formatting are generally understandable but have grammatical and wording flaws. References are mostly bare URLs, the bibliography includes Wikipedia despite the task's prohibition, and some table citations are absent from the bibliography.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"The first fifteen access periods are listed above."}]}]}
